  off it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the stored user ID is [3mauthoritative[0m — something later forms send to the server as proof of who's
  acting — or merely a [3mconvenience copy[0m the server never trusts. This is the expensive one to change later, because it
  determines what every future form's request looks like and what the server does with it.

  - [1mServer-held session, client keeps a display copy[0m — login sets an httpOnly cookie; the browser attaches it
    automatically to every later request, and JS stores the [38;5;153muserId[39m only for showing "logged in as X" and for your
    tracking. Later forms send nothing extra. Costs a real server-side session (the stub at [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m doesn't have one
    yet). Rules out nothing.
  - [1mClient stores the ID and sends it with each request[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m/[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, every future form reads it and
    includes it. No server session needed, works against the stub today. But any script on the page can read or forge
    it, so the server can't use it for authorization — it's a tracking tag wearing an identity's clothes. Migrating off
    it later means touching every form that reads it.
  - [1mIn-memory only, re-fetched on load[0m — a module-level value, gone on reload until a [38;5;153m/me[39m call restores it. Cleanest
    invariant, no stale identity. Requires an endpoint the stub doesn't have, and a loading state on every page.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m You said "persist," and a cookie persists without making the identity forgeable — the
  client copy stays a label, never a credential. It keeps the property I flagged last turn: identity is something login
  [3mestablishes[0m, not something a caller asserts. The second option looks cheaper today and is the one that's genuinely
  hard to walk back, because the cost lands spread across every form you add between now and then.
